You are writing a prompt for an AI coding agent.
Produce a single self-contained prompt that tells the agent how to recreate a
Python command-line tool from scratch.
Describe the tool's purpose, inputs, outputs, dependencies, key logic, error
handling, edge cases, and usage.
Do not include code or code snippets.
The tool is a Termux-oriented (Android, `/data/data/com.termux` shebang) Python
CLI that scans a directory tree for executable-like files or binaries (or a
given extension set) and probes each one by running it with a set of
help/version flags to detect whether it can load its shared libraries.
It is intended to find broken binaries and report missing shared-library or "not
found" errors.
Please write the prompt so the agent rebuilds the complete script, including: -
A Termux-compatible Python 3 shebang, `from __future__ import annotations`, and
only standard-library imports (argparse, concurrent.futures, ctypes, logging,
os, shutil, subprocess, sys, time, pathlib, typing). - A set of directory names
to skip during traversal (version control folders, caches, virtualenvs, build
output, node_modules), and a path check that skips any path containing those
parts. - Recognition of missing-library error messages in command output (shared
object loading failures, "not found", "no such file", "failed to load") and the
probe flags to try in order (--help, -h, --version, -v, --info). - A colored
console print helper that uses ANSI colors only when stdout is a TTY. - A
unique-path helper that appends a numeric suffix before the extension when a
file already exists. - A recursive file iterator with an extension filter,
skipping ignored directories. - Concurrent probing via a thread or process pool,
with per-probe timeouts, and a time measurement of the total run. - A
command-line interface built with argparse that accepts a root path, extension
filters, worker count, timeout, output/report file, and verbosity, with logging
configured accordingly. - Use of shutil.which or PATH lookup to locate
executables, and subprocess calls with captured output, handling
FileNotFoundError, PermissionError, TimeoutExpired, and OSError gracefully. -
Optional use of ctypes for loading shared libraries to verify them, where
relevant. - Exit codes: zero when everything probes cleanly, non-zero when
failures are detected, and a clean exit on KeyboardInterrupt. - A summary
printed at the end with counts of OK, failed, skipped, and timed-out items, plus
a per-item report of missing libraries.
Instruct the agent to write idiomatic, well-commented code matching this
description, keep it a single file, and verify the script runs with `python
script.py --help`. ---
